_SHADER_AI_PROMPT = """Write a generative GLSL ES 3.00 fragment shader for Gravewright.

RESPONSE FORMAT
Return helper functions and `void main()` only. Do not include `#version`, `precision`, uniform/input/output declarations, Markdown fences, or explanations. The engine prepends its preamble and compiles the text as a fragment shader.

RENDERING CONTRACT
The shader paints a transparent frame over the map. It cannot read the scene image. The frame is already sized to the effect range and clipped by range, walls, and doors; do not reproduce that clipping in GLSL. Always output premultiplied alpha: `finalColor = vec4(colour * a, a)`. The engine applies Normal, Add, Multiply, or Screen afterward; do not implement blending in the shader.

ALREADY AVAILABLE: DO NOT DECLARE
  in vec2 vTextureCoord;
  out vec4 finalColor;
  uniform sampler2D uTexture;      // white frame texture, not the map
  uniform sampler2D uLightBuffer;  // accumulated scene lighting
  uniform float uTime;             // seconds, wraps hourly
  uniform float uIntensity;        // 0..1, internal effect strength
  uniform float uOpacity;          // 0..1, automatically applied after main()
  uniform float uScale;            // 0.1..20
  uniform float uSpeed;            // 0..8
  uniform vec3 uColor;             // channels 0..1
  uniform vec2 uResolution;        // frame size in pixels
  uniform float uAspect;
  uniform vec2 uOrigin;            // world-space origin
  uniform float uRadius;           // world-space range; 0 = whole scene
  uniform float uRotation;         // radians
  uniform vec3 uCamera;            // x/y offset and zoom
  uniform vec2 uScreen;            // screen size in pixels
  vec2 gwScreen(vec2 uv);
  vec2 gwScreenUV(vec2 uv);
  vec2 gwWorld(vec2 uv);           // stable under pan and zoom
  vec2 gwRotated(vec2 uv);         // world point rotated around uOrigin
  float gwFeature();               // characteristic size tied to range and uScale
  vec2 gwPattern(vec2 uv);         // (gwRotated(uv) - uOrigin) / gwFeature(); returns (0,0) at the click
  vec4 gwLight(vec2 uv);           // rgb = light colour; a = strength

RULES
1. Build patterns on `gwPattern(vTextureCoord)`: it returns exactly `vec2(0.0)` at the clicked point, which is always the effect centre/start. Use `gwWorld`/`gwRotated` only for absolute world coordinates. Do not use raw `vTextureCoord` for map-anchored patterns, and do not subtract `uOrigin` from `gwPattern` again.
2. Use `uTime * uSpeed` for animation, `uIntensity` for internal strength/alpha, and `uColor` as the dominant colour. `uScale` already affects `gwFeature()`; do not apply it twice when using `gwPattern`.
3. The engine multiplies `finalColor` by `uOpacity` after `main()`. Do not multiply by `uOpacity`, or transparency will be applied twice.
4. `gwLight(vTextureCoord)` is optional. Use it when the requested effect should react to lighting; do not make an effect disappear in darkness unless requested.
5. GLSL ES does not broadcast addition/subtraction: never add `vec2 + float` or `vec3 + float`. Convert explicitly, for example `p + vec2(t)` or `colour + vec3(value)`. Vector–scalar multiplication is valid.
6. In `smoothstep(edge0, edge1, x)`, keep `edge0 < edge1`. To invert it, use `1.0 - smoothstep(smaller, larger, x)`; reversed edges have undefined results.
7. Avoid division by zero, NaN/Inf, unbounded loops, and excessive per-pixel work. Up to 8 shaders may run together; source is limited to 32,000 characters.

DESIRED EFFECT
<describe appearance, motion, density, and lighting behaviour here>"""

CATALOG["lighting.shaders.prompt"] = _SHADER_AI_PROMPT
CATALOG["lighting.shaders.prompt_prose"] = _SHADER_AI_PROMPT
